WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:05.540
9 years ago, when Facebook bought Instagram
for $1 billion, many industry leaders were

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:07.550
skeptical about the decision.

00:00:07.550 --> 00:00:11.940
Facebook themselves had just become profitable
a few years before that, and they hadn’t

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:13.419
even gone public yet.

00:00:13.419 --> 00:00:18.680
So, it didn’t seem very reasonable to spend
$1 billion on a photo-sharing app that had

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:22.920
only been around for 1.5 years with a total
of 13 employees.

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:27.609
With time, however, Facebook’s decision
to acquire Instagram has proven to be a brilliant

00:00:27.609 --> 00:00:33.800
business decision as Instagram is estimated
to be worth a whopping $100 billion today.

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:38.840
Given that Facebook didn’t create Instagram
though, what happened to Instagram’s founders

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:41.640
and why did they leave Facebook in 2018?

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:48.530
Well, starting off with Mike Krieger, Mike
was born on March 4, 1986, in Sao Paulo, Brazil.

00:00:48.530 --> 00:00:52.440
We don’t really know much about his early
life, but it does seem like Mike excelled

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:56.719
in school as he would be admitted into Stanford
in 2004.

00:00:56.719 --> 00:01:01.470
At Stanford, Mike majored in symbolic systems
and he would later meet Kevin Systrom, the

00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:04.320
other founder of Instagram, during a fellowship.

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:08.420
When the two eventually started working on
Instagram, Mike mainly managed the technical

00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:09.980
side of Instagram.

00:01:09.980 --> 00:01:14.700
He would fix outages, make sure there was
enough server capacity, and so on and so forth.

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:19.230
One time, Mike says that when he woke up in
the morning, he saw an email saying that Instagram

00:01:19.230 --> 00:01:20.630
had gone down.

00:01:20.630 --> 00:01:24.920
He instantly opened up the app to see what
was going on, when he noticed that Instagram

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:26.890
was running perfectly fine.

00:01:26.890 --> 00:01:31.259
Mike asked Kevin and some early employees
if they had fixed the issues, but none of

00:01:31.259 --> 00:01:32.290
them had.

00:01:32.290 --> 00:01:36.490
Confused, Mike decided to look at the terminal
to see who had made the last edit, and it

00:01:36.490 --> 00:01:41.200
turns out that Mike had actually woken up
at 3:30 AM, fixed the outage, and gone back

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:42.329
to sleep.

00:01:42.329 --> 00:01:46.860
Clearly, Instagram’s operations weren’t
perfect, but this would all change when Facebook

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:49.330
bought Instagram in late 2012.

00:01:49.330 --> 00:01:54.790
Owning 10% of the company, Mike made a total
of $100 million from the acquisition.

00:01:54.790 --> 00:01:58.659
Assuming that Mike’s payout was proportional
to the overall acquisition, Mike received

00:01:58.659 --> 00:02:02.020
$30 million in cash and $70 million in Facebook
stock.

00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:06.770
Facebook decided to keep Instagram a separately
run business, so Mike and Kevin maintained

00:02:06.770 --> 00:02:08.229
relative autonomy.

00:02:08.229 --> 00:02:12.440
But the financial, technological, and human
resources they received from Facebook were

00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:13.990
invaluable.

00:02:13.990 --> 00:02:18.530
After the acquisition, Mike became the CTO
of Instagram where he scaled the engineering

00:02:18.530 --> 00:02:22.510
team from just 6 general developers to 300
specialized engineers.

00:02:22.510 --> 00:02:26.530
One of the first projects that the team worked
on after the acquisition was launching a web

00:02:26.530 --> 00:02:28.560
browser version of Instagram.

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:32.670
This was one of the first times that a mobile
app was translated into a website.

00:02:32.670 --> 00:02:35.330
Usually, it was the other way around.

00:02:35.330 --> 00:02:40.940
Anyway, later in April of 2015, Mike partnered
with a charity organization called GiveWell,

00:02:40.940 --> 00:02:42.810
where he donated $750,000.

00:02:42.810 --> 00:02:48.950
In 2016, Mike worked on redesigning the app
to better match modern UI and design.

00:02:48.950 --> 00:02:52.820
And in 2018, Mike worked on launching IGTV.

00:02:52.820 --> 00:02:55.230
At this point, it seemed like things were
going great.

00:02:55.230 --> 00:02:58.690
Mike had a lot of autonomy within Instagram,
the app’s user base was growing exponentially,

00:02:58.690 --> 00:03:03.140
and Instagram was transitioning from an up-and-coming
startup to an established social media player.

00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:08.650
Yet, just a couple of months after the launch
of IGTV in September of 2018, Mike and Kevin

00:03:08.650 --> 00:03:11.980
announced that they would be leaving Instagram
and Facebook.

00:03:11.980 --> 00:03:15.550
But before we get into that, let’s take
a look at Kevin’s side of the story up until

00:03:15.550 --> 00:03:16.550
this point.

00:03:16.550 --> 00:03:21.380
Kevin Systrom was born on December 30, 1983,
in Holliston Massachusetts.

00:03:21.380 --> 00:03:25.520
As a kid, Kevin often played a video game
called Doom 2 which fueled an interest in

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:26.520
programming.

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:30.960
Kevin would also end up attending Stanford
for his bachelor’s, but Kevin majored in

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:33.150
management science and engineering.

00:03:33.150 --> 00:03:37.540
While at Stanford, Kevin was selected to take
part in the Mayfield Fellows Program which

00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:41.130
not only allowed him to meet Mike but also
score an internship at Odeo.

00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:45.580
If you’re not familiar with Odeo, these
are actually the guys who would go on to create

00:03:45.580 --> 00:03:46.580
Twitter.

00:03:46.580 --> 00:03:51.090
Anyway, Kevin didn’t jump straight into
Instagram, or Burbn as it was originally called,

00:03:51.090 --> 00:03:52.310
right after college.

00:03:52.310 --> 00:03:57.430
In 2006, Kevin actually joined Google as an
associate product marketing manager.

00:03:57.430 --> 00:04:01.790
At Google, Kevin worked on Google calendar,
Gmail, Docs, and Sheets.

00:04:01.790 --> 00:04:05.950
A few years into his venture at Google, a
couple of his coworkers left the company to

00:04:05.950 --> 00:04:08.480
create their own company called Nextstop.

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:12.270
Nextstop was a social travel recommendation
site, and Kevin would follow his coworkers

00:04:12.270 --> 00:04:13.750
and join the startup.

00:04:13.750 --> 00:04:18.200
Kevin didn’t work at Nextstop for too long,
but his experience with the startup was essential

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:19.709
in the creation of Instagram.

00:04:19.709 --> 00:04:24.300
Fun fact, Nextstop was acquired by Facebook
as well in 2010.

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:27.990
After his stint at Nextstop, Kevin would go
around silicon valley and pitch his idea for

00:04:27.990 --> 00:04:30.400
Burbn to a plethora of venture capitalists.

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:35.700
Andreessen Horowitz and Baseline Ventures
would agree to invest $500,000 into the idea

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:39.600
and this allowed Kevin and Mike to get Burbn
off the ground.

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:43.650
Initially, Burbn was actually a check-in app
that allowed users to share their location,

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:45.810
post pictures, and accumulate points.

00:04:45.810 --> 00:04:50.860
However, Kevin soon decided that it was better
to just focus on one feature and this led

00:04:50.860 --> 00:04:53.210
to the creation of Instagram.

00:04:53.210 --> 00:04:57.950
Instagram was a rather trendy launch as the
app basically went viral immediately.

00:04:57.950 --> 00:05:03.990
Within the first 24 hours, 25,000 people signed
up for Instagram and within 9 months, Instagram

00:05:03.990 --> 00:05:07.050
boasted a total of 7 million users.

00:05:07.050 --> 00:05:12.060
Meanwhile, Facebook’s user growth had stalled
and many users had even stopped using Facebook.

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:16.090
So, Mark Zuckerberg made a gutsy move to play
it big with Instagram.

00:05:16.090 --> 00:05:21.150
Kevin also received an offer from Jack Dorsey
as well, but their offer was only half of

00:05:21.150 --> 00:05:23.740
Facebook’s offer at $500 million.

00:05:23.740 --> 00:05:28.350
Kevin also had plenty of private funding as
well which could run the company for years.

00:05:28.350 --> 00:05:32.620
But, he felt that support from Facebook would
largely streamline the process.

00:05:32.620 --> 00:05:36.390
He also didn’t want to lose control though,
so he put in a contingency that Instagram

00:05:36.390 --> 00:05:37.930
would operate as an independent company.

00:05:37.930 --> 00:05:42.380
As we all know, Facebook would agree and Kevin
would remain the CEO of Instagram under the

00:05:42.380 --> 00:05:44.210
ownership of Facebook.

00:05:44.210 --> 00:05:49.350
The acquisition netted Kevin a total of $400
million, and if we assume that the ratio of

00:05:49.350 --> 00:05:55.420
cash to stock is the same as the overall acquisition,
Kevin received $120 million in cash and $280

00:05:55.420 --> 00:05:57.320
million in Facebook stock.

00:05:57.320 --> 00:06:01.510
Under Kevin’s leadership, we saw the release
of a variety of different filters.

00:06:01.510 --> 00:06:05.060
The story goes that Kevin’s girlfriend didn’t
want to post pictures from their vacation

00:06:05.060 --> 00:06:08.590
to Mexico because she didn’t like the quality
of the pictures.

00:06:08.590 --> 00:06:12.430
Kevin’s solution to this conundrum was developing
filters that would effectively hide the disguise

00:06:12.430 --> 00:06:13.889
the quality of the pictures.

00:06:13.889 --> 00:06:18.000
Nowadays, this isn’t really that big of
a concern considering how advanced smartphone

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:19.460
cameras have become.

00:06:19.460 --> 00:06:23.410
Nonetheless, filters have become a staple
of Instagram today.

00:06:23.410 --> 00:06:27.930
Aside from filters, Kevin also oversaw the
release of the explore tab and the monetization

00:06:27.930 --> 00:06:29.139
of Instagram.

00:06:29.139 --> 00:06:33.540
And in May of 2017, Kevin described how he
envisioned Instagram getting involved with

00:06:33.540 --> 00:06:36.370
virtual reality products within the next few
years.

00:06:36.370 --> 00:06:40.180
So clearly, he had no intentions of leaving
the company at this time.

00:06:40.180 --> 00:06:43.170
Yet, he would do just that a year later.

00:06:43.170 --> 00:06:44.570
So what happened?

00:06:44.570 --> 00:06:50.810
Well, neither Mike, Kevin, nor Mark have really
pointed any fingers at each other publicly.

00:06:50.810 --> 00:06:54.260
Mark simply congratulated Mike and Kevin on
their success and wished them good luck in

00:06:54.260 --> 00:06:55.550
their future endeavors.

00:06:55.550 --> 00:06:59.980
Meanwhile, Mike and Kevin simply stated that
they were ready to move onto new ventures.

00:06:59.980 --> 00:07:04.360
In reality, it seems like there was much more
going on behind the scenes.

00:07:04.360 --> 00:07:08.730
You see, though Instagram’s autonomy allowed
Kevin and Mike to retain control of Instagram

00:07:08.730 --> 00:07:13.050
and grow it how they wanted, it also created
a divide within Facebook.

00:07:13.050 --> 00:07:17.340
A lot of Facebook’s executives felt that
Instagram was outshining Facebook and that

00:07:17.340 --> 00:07:20.200
Facebook should get more credit for Instagram’s
success.

00:07:20.200 --> 00:07:24.410
I don’t Mark was part of this group as I
think he was proud and happy that he made

00:07:24.410 --> 00:07:25.530
a great call with Instagram.

00:07:25.530 --> 00:07:30.460
But, he didn’t want to upset the executives
that were jealous, so he started to impose

00:07:30.460 --> 00:07:33.990
limitations on Instagram and take more credit
for Instagram’s success.

00:07:33.990 --> 00:07:38.810
In the July 2018 earnings call, for instance,
Mark stated quote “We believe Insta­gram

00:07:38.810 --> 00:07:42.550
has been able to use Facebook’s infrastructure
to grow more than twice as quickly as it would

00:07:42.550 --> 00:07:44.150
have on its own.”

00:07:44.150 --> 00:07:48.100
Soon after, Mark asked his head of growth
to make a list of all the ways Facebook supported

00:07:48.100 --> 00:07:50.210
and boosted Instagram’s growth.

00:07:50.210 --> 00:07:54.070
Mark would then go ahead and inform Kevin
and Mike that he has to take away all of these

00:07:54.070 --> 00:07:57.389
supports in order to protect the Facebook
app.

00:07:57.389 --> 00:08:01.910
Around the same time, Kevin participated in
some magazine profiles, and Mark would go

00:08:01.910 --> 00:08:06.990
ahead and ban Facebook employees from participating
in magazine profiles without approval from

00:08:06.990 --> 00:08:10.440
himself or Sheryl Sandberg who is the COO
of Facebook.

00:08:10.440 --> 00:08:14.650
Overwhelmed by all of these new rules, Kevin
would use paternity leave as an excuse to

00:08:14.650 --> 00:08:16.840
take a break from Instagram for a little bit.

00:08:16.840 --> 00:08:19.040
But Mark continued to push Kevin’s buttons.

00:08:19.040 --> 00:08:23.680
While Kevin was on leave, Mark started testing
location tracking on Instagram, something

00:08:23.680 --> 00:08:25.790
Kevin was strongly against.

00:08:25.790 --> 00:08:29.240
By this point, Kevin became convinced that
Mark was trying to make Kevin’s life as

00:08:29.240 --> 00:08:32.960
miserable as possible in order to force him
to resign.

00:08:32.960 --> 00:08:37.240
This was also around the same time that Facebook
was involved in the Cambridge Analytica scandal

00:08:37.240 --> 00:08:39.360
which just further put off Kevin and Mike.

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:43.450
So, the duo took the hint and resigned from
Instagram shortly after Kevin returned from

00:08:43.450 --> 00:08:44.740
paternity leave.

00:08:44.740 --> 00:08:48.890
Adam Mosseri took over as the new leader of
the company, and Instagram has been doing

00:08:48.890 --> 00:08:50.700
just fine since then.

00:08:50.700 --> 00:08:55.240
However, it does seem like Instagram more
or less lost the autonomy that it once had.

00:08:55.240 --> 00:08:59.830
Kevin would eventually hint at this tension
between himself and Mark by stating “No

00:08:59.830 --> 00:09:02.250
one ever leaves a job because everything’s
awesome.”

00:09:02.250 --> 00:09:07.260
Anyway, since their departure, Kevin and Mike
have taken it easy opting to focus on their

00:09:07.260 --> 00:09:10.070
families, hobbies, and self-improvement.

00:09:10.070 --> 00:09:14.550
The biggest thing the duo has embarked on
since leaving Instagram has been rt.live.

00:09:14.550 --> 00:09:19.370
The duo launched the website in April of 2020,
and the website provided up-to-date data as

00:09:19.370 --> 00:09:22.370
to how fast Covid 19 was spreading in each
state.

00:09:22.370 --> 00:09:26.680
If you go to rt.live now though, you’ll
see that the website has been shut down due

00:09:26.680 --> 00:09:28.540
to better tracking metrics being available.

00:09:28.540 --> 00:09:32.310
And that’s pretty much it from Mike and
Kevin.

00:09:32.310 --> 00:09:40.440
After the acquisition, the duo took on leading
roles at Instagram and made Instagram a leading

00:09:40.440 --> 00:09:41.440
social media company.

00:09:41.440 --> 00:09:42.589
For many years, Instagram’s expansion was
going great.

00:09:42.589 --> 00:09:45.950
However, their explosive success ended up
being the root of their downfall.

00:09:45.950 --> 00:09:51.500
Instagram’s autonomy began to slowly erode,
and eventually, Kevin and Mike had enough

00:09:51.500 --> 00:09:52.790
and they left the company.

00:09:52.790 --> 00:09:54.540
It’s not all bad though.

00:09:54.540 --> 00:09:58.610
Since then, they’ve been taking it easy,
finally enjoying the wealth they earned years

00:09:58.610 --> 00:09:59.610
ago.

00:09:59.610 --> 00:10:02.740
Do you guys think Facebook’s acquisition
of Instagram was a positive or a negative

00:10:02.740 --> 00:10:03.740
long-term?

